<h2>A commercial quarrel that became a criminal case</h2>
<p>The complainant, chief executive of a construction firm, worked as a sub-contractor on four sites for LandTech AEPC Pvt Ltd. The dispute crystallised around one document: a &ldquo;revised work order&rdquo; dated 30 August 2022, sent to him by email on 6 May 2023, which reduced the contract value of one site. He alleged that his seal and signature on it were forged, and claimed dues of about Rs 9.90 crore. The city police&rsquo;s Central Crime Branch had earlier closed his complaint as a civil dispute; the jurisdictional Magistrate thereafter directed registration of an FIR under Section 175(3) BNSS, and a challenge to that direction failed before the High Court in August 2026. Crime No. 143 of 2026 followed, invoking Sections 420, 468 and 471 IPC for the pre-2024 events.</p>

<h2>The Court&rsquo;s framework: custody, not culpability</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Seven-year ceiling.</strong> Every offence alleged carries a maximum of seven years, the band in which arrest &ldquo;does not follow as a matter of course&rdquo; — the Arnesh Kumar discipline, now carried into Section 35 BNSS.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>What earlier orders decided.</strong> The Magistrate&rsquo;s direction to register the FIR, and the High Court&rsquo;s refusal to quash it, settled only that cognizable offences were disclosed — not that the investigation required custody.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The document test.</strong> The case rests on one document the accused admit sending. Its genuineness can be tested through the original, the device it was prepared on, email records and admitted specimens — all obtainable by directions, not detention.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No money trail to chase.</strong> The quarrel is between two contracting parties over contract value; no diverted funds remained to be traced through interrogation.</div>
</div>
<p>Justice N. Ramesh relied on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s order in <em>Miteshgiri v. State of Gujarat</em> (SLP (Crl.) No. 15785 of 2026, order dated 30.09.2026), cited in the order for the proposition that custodial interrogation is not needed where the allegations rest on documents. The intervention application filed by the complainant was allowed and his objections heard — including an argument that the accused might tamper with evidence, which the Court found unparticularised and adequately met by a no-contact condition.</p>

<h2>Conditions that do the investigation&rsquo;s work</h2>
<p>What distinguishes the order is how the bail conditions substitute for custody. The petitioners must produce, at their first appearance before the investigating officer, the original work order and the device on which it was prepared — or state in writing where these are. They must comply with any direction under Section 349 BNSS to furnish specimen signatures or handwriting. Daily attendance before the investigating officer for fifteen days keeps them available for questioning. A breach permits the Magistrate to act as if bail had been granted by that court, and absconding exposes them to a fresh case under Section 269 BNS.</p>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Investigative need</th><th>How the order meets it without custody</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Examining the disputed document</td><td>Production of the original work order at first appearance</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Tracing how it was created</td><td>Production of the device, or written disclosure of its whereabouts</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Comparing signatures</td><td>Specimens under Section 349 BNSS on the Magistrate&rsquo;s direction</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Availability for questioning</td><td>Daily appearance before the IO for fifteen days</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Witness protection</td><td>No contact with the complainant or his workmen, except through court or arbitrator</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What the Court did not decide</h2>
<p>The order is careful about its own limits. Whether the work order is in fact forged, how a document dated 2022 squares with progress entries of 2023, and whether the dispute is ultimately civil and arbitrable — all were expressly left open. The observations are prima facie and bind neither the investigation nor any trial. For accused persons facing commercial-fraud FIRs, the case illustrates a recurring and winnable argument: identify precisely what custody would add to the investigation, and show the court that directions can achieve the same thing.</p>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information only. It is not legal advice, and it does not comment on the merits of the pending investigation.</div>
